ppen by accident it's an  intentional managed process requiring  resources huge areas of land fences  water supplies managers and ecologists  and squads of armed Rangers to protect  against illegal poaching this all costs  money in a part of the world where  people are very poor it's hard to expect  people who live like this to care about  nature conservation before their basic  human needs have been met  food shelter financial security which  brings us to the second point the  biggest killer of wildlife by far  anywhere in the world is human  encroachment wild habitats are destroyed  to create farmland animals are hunted  for meat  remember in Africa many people eat  protein starve diets and cannot afford  meat  farmers in America might complain about  the occasional wolf well try being a  farmer in Africa hyenas leopards pythons  crocodiles elephants are shot because  their sample crops lions killed because  they eat livestock hippos offer danger  when they're alive we can feed a whole  village if they're dead and this problem  is only getting much bigger  Rooker's population is exploding look at  this chart like epic performance from  Africa over the next hundred years so  there has to be an economic incentive to  conserve wildlife and hunting can  provided by setting hunting quarters  below the animal population growth rate  and by targeting older male animals  hunting can be both sustainable and  profitable and that gives landowners an  incentive to convert from commercial  farming to game reserves it works since  regulated Rhino hunting was introduced  in South Africa the white rhino  population has grown tenfold the best  example is Namibia's community  Conservancy programme Namibia has gone  and given ownership and management of  wild land over to local communities  income from hunting and tourism goes  directly into conservation expenses  local communities gain income jobs meet  these conservancies now cover an area  larger than England okay so then what  about the other option for generating  income photo tourism I long for the day  when tourism can carry the weight of  sustaining Africa's wild areas but we're  a long way off from that tourism is a  large industry but it's concentrated in  a few areas it needs to be safe and  close to airports  whereas trophy hunters will go anywhere  and pay top dollar to pursue their  passion let's look again at those  Namibian conservancies they're clustered  up in the north of the country and on  this map they've been color-coded to  show which of them are currently  profitable green and which are lost  making red now let's simulate what would  happen if we remove the benefits of  hunting the map changes green to red and  those red areas they go back to what  they were 30 years ago cattle farms land  over graze to dust  so you see hunting is sustaining remote  wild places that many people have never  heard of and now now hunting can help  pay for protection against the new  crisis the rampant illegal wildlife  trade driven  I demand from China and Vietnam for  rhino horn ivory and lion burn rider  horn is now worth more than cocaine or  gold and that means these incredible  animals having their faces hacked off  sometimes while they're still alive and  this this must never be okay we have to  be better than this  unlike hunting this killing is  unregulated unsustainable and totally  out of control  and we need every conservation tool  available to prevent it now not all  hunting is good as in any system there  are problems and abuses and canned  hunting is one of those animals often  lions bred in captivity so that they can  be shot in staged tonnes these lines are  tame they can't even hunt for themselves  they're taken away from their mothers  when they're three days old so that the  mothers will breed again and again real  lions are raised by their mothers for at  least 18 months hunters need to take  responsibility for stopping this because  it's their dollars they keep it going  other hunting systems may be run with  incompetence or corruption meaning the  benefits don't flow to local people but  this means we need to seek improvement  not shut the whole thing down and right  now this is real since that's all the  line 45 airlines have bound the  transport of animal trophies and several  Western governments are considering  import bans the whole thing is under  threat and as we saw in Namibia a  knee-jerk uninformed ban could lead to  the loss of huge areas of wild land and  then what about the 184 thousand people  who live on Olivia's conservancies who's  thinking of them what about African  citizens of other countries who live  with and support trophy hunting almost  all the anti hunting backlash comes from  wealthy people it's it's so ironic it  sometimes feels like the two things that  Facebook warriors around the world care  about when it comes to Africa our human  poverty and wild animals  yet the anti-hunting campaign could hurt  both human poverty and wild animals so I  dislike hunting but the majesty of  Africa's wildlife has been one of the  highlights of my life I must do what I  can to preserve it for future  generations that means making hard and  informed choices and it may also mean  having to cooperate and coexist with  those who are different from me those  who might may never understand in  pursuit of a shared goal I hope that  this talk has helped you to see some of  the complexities of conservation and how  hunting can play a role and finally I  really hope that everyone in this room  one day has the chance to come to Africa  how's the holiday of a lifetime live to  the rhythm of nature shoot with a camera  not with a gun you will support jobs and  conservation and if enough of you do  that one day we won't need to kill to  protect thank you  [Music]  you  [Music]

